  "solutions": [
    {
      "title": "Brute Force",
      "complexity": "Time: O(n\u00b2) | Space: O(n)",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function twoSumSorted(numbers, target) {\n  const arr = numbers.slice().sort((a, b) => a - b);\n  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {\n    for (let j = arr.length - 1; j > i; j--) {\n      if (arr[i] + arr[j] === target) {\n        return [i + 1, j + 1];\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  return [];\n}",
      "isReference": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Optimal",
      "complexity": "Time: O(n log n) | Space: O(n)",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function twoSumSorted(numbers, target) {\n  const arr = numbers.slice().sort((a, b) => a - b);\n  let left = 0;\n  let right = arr.length - 1;\n  while (left < right) {\n    const sum = arr[left] + arr[right];\n    if (sum === target) {\n      return [left + 1, right + 1];\n    }\n    if (sum < target) {\n      left++;\n    } else {\n      right--;\n    }\n  }\n  return [];\n}",
      "isReference": true
    },
    {
      "title": "Brute Force",
      "complexity": "Time: O(n\u00b2) | Space: O(n)",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def twoSumSorted(numbers, target):\n    arr = sorted(numbers[:])\n    for i in range(len(arr)):\n        for j in range(len(arr) - 1, i, -1):\n            if arr[i] + arr[j] == target:\n                return [i + 1, j + 1]\n    return []\n",
      "isReference": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Optimal",
      "complexity": "Time: O(n log n) | Space: O(n)",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def twoSumSorted(numbers, target):\n    arr = sorted(numbers[:])\n    left = 0\n    right = len(arr) - 1\n    while left < right:\n        s = arr[left] + arr[right]\n        if s == target:\n            return [left + 1, right + 1]\n        if s < target:\n            left += 1\n        else:\n            right -= 1\n    return []\n",
      "isReference": true
    }
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute-force approach sorts the array and uses nested loops to find the first pair (outermost indices) summing to the target, yielding O(n^2) time. The optimal approach sorts once and then uses the two-pointer technique: start with the smallest and largest elements; if the sum is too small, move the left pointer up; if too large, move the right pointer down.",
    "why_optimal": "The two-pointer scan is O(n) after the O(n log n) sort, versus O(n^2) for nested loops. On 22 test cases, PAF measured the brute-force at 1.09 ms and the optimal at 0.27 ms\u2014a 4.0x speedup that grows quadratically with input size.",
    "pitfalls": "The two-pointer technique only works because the array is sorted beforehand. The first pair found is always the outermost (smallest left, largest right) because that's where the pointers start. Returning 0-based indices instead of 1-based. Returning a pair when no solution exists instead of an empty array."
  },
